{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-336278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg}$ and the Moon has a mass of $(7.35\\times {10}^{22})\\,\\text{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of Earth and the Moon is $(3.84\\times {10}^{8})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between Earth and the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between Earth and the Moon is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Earth and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of the Moon. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\cdot (7.35\\times {10}^{22})}{(3.84\\times {10}^{8})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 5.97\\cdot 7.35}{(3.84)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}\\cdot {10}^{22}}{({10}^{8})^{2}} \\\\  & =(19.848\\,37…)\\times {10}^{19} \\\\  & =(1.984\\,837…)\\times {10}^{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between Earth and the Moon is\n$(\\,1.98\\times {10}^{20}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f363e2f91d00e5f3f4397d29567877f892e83cbc9f4238d3d210cb85fcb42a18"} {"question" "q-336278" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336278" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.98"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336278" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336278" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{20}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Saturn has a mass of $(5.68\\times {10}^{26})\\,\\text{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of the Sun and Saturn is $(1.43\\times {10}^{12})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the Sun and Saturn? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the Sun and Saturn is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Saturn. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (5.68\\times {10}^{26})}{(1.43\\times {10}^{12})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 5.68}{(1.43)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{26}}{({10}^{12})^{2}} \\\\  & =(36.868\\,47…)\\times {10}^{21} \\\\  & =(3.686\\,847…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between the Sun and Saturn is\n$(\\,3.69\\times {10}^{22}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336280" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f363e2f91d00e5f3f4397d29567877f892e83cbc9f4238d3d210cb85fcb42a18"} {"question" "q-336280" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336280" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3.69"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336280" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336280" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{22}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336321" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Uranus has a mass of $(8.68\\times {10}^{25})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Neptune has a mass of $(1.02\\times {10}^{26})\\,\\text{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of Uranus and Neptune is $(1.63\\times {10}^{12})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between Uranus and Neptune? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between Uranus and Neptune is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336321" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Uranus and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Neptune. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(8.68\\times {10}^{25})\\cdot (1.02\\times {10}^{26})}{(1.63\\times {10}^{12})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 8.68\\cdot 1.02}{(1.63)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{25}\\cdot {10}^{26}}{({10}^{12})^{2}} \\\\  & =(22.226\\,47…)\\times {10}^{16} \\\\  & =(2.222\\,647…)\\times {10}^{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between Uranus and Neptune is\n$(\\,2.22\\times {10}^{17}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336321" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f363e2f91d00e5f3f4397d29567877f892e83cbc9f4238d3d210cb85fcb42a18"} {"question" "q-336321" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336321" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.22"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336321" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336321" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{17}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The centers of two objects of masses $(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(4.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ are separated by a distance of $(7.5\\times {10}^{2})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(4.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\cdot (4.2\\times {10}^{6})}{(7.5\\times {10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 6.5\\cdot 4.2}{(7.5)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{({10}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =3.237\\,173…\\times {10}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects is\n$(\\,3.2\\times {10}^{-4}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f363e2f91d00e5f3f4397d29567877f892e83cbc9f4238d3d210cb85fcb42a18"} {"question" "q-336275" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336275" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3.2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336275" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336275" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{-4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The centers of two objects of masses $(3.4\\times {10}^{3})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(9.0\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ are separated by a distance of $(2.5\\times {10}^{3})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(3.4\\times {10}^{3})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(9.0\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(3.4\\times {10}^{3})\\cdot (9.0\\times {10}^{7})}{(2.5\\times {10}^{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 3.4\\cdot 9.0}{(2.5)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{3}\\cdot {10}^{7}}{({10}^{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =32.656\\,32\\times {10}^{-7} \\\\  & =3.265\\,632\\times {10}^{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects is\n$(\\,3.3\\times {10}^{-6}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f363e2f91d00e5f3f4397d29567877f892e83cbc9f4238d3d210cb85fcb42a18"} {"question" "q-336273" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336273" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3.3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336273" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336273" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{-6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(5.0\\times {10}^{3})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(7.0\\times {10}^{-6})\\,\\text{N}.$ If one object has mass $(2.0\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of the other object is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{2}$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(7.0\\times {10}^{-6})\\cdot (5.0\\times {10}^{3})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (2.0\\times {10}^{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7.0\\cdot (5.0)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 2.0}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-6}\\cdot ({10}^{3})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}} \\\\  & =(13.118…)\\times {10}^{6} \\\\  & =(1.3118…)\\times {10}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$(\\,1.3\\times {10}^{7}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f363e2f91d00e5f3f4397d29567877f892e83cbc9f4238d3d210cb85fcb42a18"} {"question" "q-336291" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336291" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336291" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336291" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{7}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336284" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(7.2\\times {10}^{2})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(5.6\\times {10}^{-4})\\,\\text{N}.$ If one object has mass $(4.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of the other object is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336284" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{2}$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(5.6\\times {10}^{-4})\\cdot (7.2\\times {10}^{2})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (4.8\\times {10}^{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5.6\\cdot (7.2)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 4.8}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-4}\\cdot ({10}^{2})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}} \\\\  & =(9.067…)\\times {10}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$(\\,9.1\\times {10}^{5}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336284" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f363e2f91d00e5f3f4397d29567877f892e83cbc9f4238d3d210cb85fcb42a18"} {"question" "q-336284" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336284" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9.1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336284" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336284" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The average distance between the centers of the Sun and Mars is $(2.28\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.64\\times {10}^{21})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of the Sun is $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of Mars is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Mars.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(1.64\\times {10}^{21})\\cdot (2.28\\times {10}^{11})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (1.99\\times {10}^{30})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.64\\cdot (2.28)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 1.99}\\times \\frac{{10}^{21}\\cdot ({10}^{11})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}} \\\\  & =(0.642\\,29…)\\times {10}^{24} \\\\  & =(6.4229…)\\times {10}^{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of Mars is\n$(\\,6.42\\times {10}^{23}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f363e2f91d00e5f3f4397d29567877f892e83cbc9f4238d3d210cb85fcb42a18"} {"question" "q-336295" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336295" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6.42"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336295" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336295" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{23}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336297" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The average distance between the centers of the Sun and Venus is $(1.08\\times {10}^{11})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(5.54\\times {10}^{22})\\,\\text{N}.$ Given that the mass of the Sun is $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of Venus? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of Venus is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336297" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Venus.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(5.54\\times {10}^{22})\\cdot (1.08\\times {10}^{11})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (1.99\\times {10}^{30})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5.54\\cdot (1.08)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 1.99}\\times \\frac{{10}^{22}\\cdot ({10}^{11})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}} \\\\  & =(0.486\\,83…)\\times {10}^{25} \\\\  & =(4.8683…)\\times {10}^{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of Venus is\n$(\\,4.87\\times {10}^{24}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336297" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f363e2f91d00e5f3f4397d29567877f892e83cbc9f4238d3d210cb85fcb42a18"} {"question" "q-336297" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336297" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4.87"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336297" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336297" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{24}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(5.0\\times {10}^{2})\\,\\text{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(7.2\\times {10}^{-5})\\,\\text{N}.$ If one object has mass $(3.6\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe mass of the other object is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{kg}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nLet ${m}_{2}$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make ${m}_{2}$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(7.2\\times {10}^{-5})\\cdot (5.0\\times {10}^{2})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (3.6\\times {10}^{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7.2\\cdot (5.0)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 3.6}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-5}\\cdot ({10}^{2})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}} \\\\  & =(7.496…)\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$(\\,7.5\\times {10}^{4}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f363e2f91d00e5f3f4397d29567877f892e83cbc9f4238d3d210cb85fcb42a18"} {"question" "q-336288" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336288" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7.5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336288" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336288" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Venus has a mass of $(4.87\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(5.54\\times {10}^{22})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the Sun and Venus? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the Sun and Venus is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Venus. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (4.87\\times {10}^{24})}{5.54\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 4.87}{5.54}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{24}}{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(11.668…)\\times {10}^{21} \\\\  & =(1.1668…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{1.1668…\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.1668…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(1.0801…)\\times {10}^{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between the Sun and Venus is\n$(\\,1.08\\times {10}^{11}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f363e2f91d00e5f3f4397d29567877f892e83cbc9f4238d3d210cb85fcb42a18"} {"question" "q-336314" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336314" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.08"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336314" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336314" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{11}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Neptune has a mass of $(1.02\\times {10}^{26})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(6.69\\times {10}^{20})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the Sun and Neptune? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the Sun and Neptune is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of the Sun and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Neptune. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (1.02\\times {10}^{26})}{6.69\\times {10}^{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 1.02}{6.69}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{26}}{{10}^{20}} \\\\  & =(2.0237…)\\times {10}^{25} \\\\  & =(20.237…)\\times {10}^{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{20.237…\\times {10}^{24}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20.237…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{24}} \\\\  & =(4.498…)\\times {10}^{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between the Sun and Neptune is\n$(\\,4.50\\times {10}^{12}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f363e2f91d00e5f3f4397d29567877f892e83cbc9f4238d3d210cb85fcb42a18"} {"question" "q-336307" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336307" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4.50"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336307" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336307" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{12}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(2.7\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(1.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(7.5\\times {10}^{-6})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(2.7\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(1.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(2.7\\times {10}^{7})\\cdot (1.8\\times {10}^{6})}{7.5\\times {10}^{-6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 2.7\\cdot 1.8}{7.5}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{7}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{{10}^{-6}} \\\\  & =4.32216\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{4.32216\\times {10}^{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4.32216}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{8}} \\\\  & =(2.078…)\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,2.1\\times {10}^{4}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336304" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f363e2f91d00e5f3f4397d29567877f892e83cbc9f4238d3d210cb85fcb42a18"} {"question" "q-336304" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336304" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336304" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336304" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(3.1\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(7.4\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(1.0\\times {10}^{-5})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(3.1\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(7.4\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(3.1\\times {10}^{6})\\cdot (7.4\\times {10}^{5})}{1.0\\times {10}^{-5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 3.1\\cdot 7.4}{1.0}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}\\cdot {10}^{5}}{{10}^{-5}} \\\\  & =153.0098\\times {10}^{5} \\\\  & =15.30098\\times {10}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{15.30098\\times {10}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15.30098}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{6}} \\\\  & =(3.9116…)\\times {10}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,3.9\\times {10}^{3}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336302" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f363e2f91d00e5f3f4397d29567877f892e83cbc9f4238d3d210cb85fcb42a18"} {"question" "q-336302" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336302" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3.9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336302" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336302" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\,\\text{kg}$ and Saturn has a mass of $(5.68\\times {10}^{26})\\,\\text{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.38\\times {10}^{17})\\,\\text{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of Earth and Saturn? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between Earth and Saturn is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet ${m}_{1}$ be the mass of Earth and ${m}_{2}$ be the mass of Saturn. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\cdot (5.68\\times {10}^{26})}{1.38\\times {10}^{17}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 5.97\\cdot 5.68}{1.38}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}\\cdot {10}^{26}}{{10}^{17}} \\\\  & =(163.8964)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{163.8964\\times {10}^{22}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{163.8964}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{22}} \\\\  & =(12.802…)\\times {10}^{11} \\\\  & =(1.2802…)\\times {10}^{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between Earth and Saturn is\n$(\\,1.28\\times {10}^{12}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f363e2f91d00e5f3f4397d29567877f892e83cbc9f4238d3d210cb85fcb42a18"} {"question" "q-336306" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336306" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.28"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336306" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "q-336306" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10^{12}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/state.json" "file_sha256" "04e62ca236f311b1461708889e1a273fcb68070c0692e0083c4fc0fc883d64c7"} {"question" "e-21744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times10^{30})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and Mars has a mass of $(6.42\\times10^{23})\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of the Sun and Mars is $(2.28\\times10^{11})\\,\\textrm{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/example-21744.json" "file_sha256" "a0e4a3c9ce013fce8d9c81f87163282c6ed2069507cc0a26830725492b419cf1"} {"question" "e-21744" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of the Sun and $m_2$ be the mass of Mars. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (6.42\\times {10}^{23})}{(2.28\\times {10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 6.42}{(2.28)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{23}}{({10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =(16.392\\,5…)\\times {10}^{20} \\\\  & =(1.639\\,25…)\\times {10}^{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars is\n$\\Big(1.64 \\times 10^{21}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{N}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/example-21744.json" "file_sha256" "a0e4a3c9ce013fce8d9c81f87163282c6ed2069507cc0a26830725492b419cf1"} {"question" "e-21745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(3.0\\times10^3)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(3.0\\times10^{-4})\\,\\textrm{N}.$ If one object has mass $(2.0\\times10^5)\\,\\textrm{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/example-21745.json" "file_sha256" "3b31876c941beb119d60aa601bf1fe47e29029ebc0e470c572ba61621eeaeec3"} {"question" "e-21745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nLet $m_2$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $m_2$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(3.0\\times {10}^{-4})\\cdot (3.0\\times {10}^{3})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (2.0\\times {10}^{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.0\\cdot (3.0)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 2.0}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-4}\\cdot ({10}^{3})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}} \\\\  & =(2.023…)\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$\\Big(2.0 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{kg}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/example-21745.json" "file_sha256" "3b31876c941beb119d60aa601bf1fe47e29029ebc0e470c572ba61621eeaeec3"} {"question" "e-21746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times10^{24})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and the Moon has a mass of $(7.35\\times10^{22})\\,\\textrm{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.98\\times10^{20})\\,\\textrm{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of Earth and the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/example-21746.json" "file_sha256" "b1c0604819defc03e32f249f87a1026e617d20952e2b7635a53bef7d52925761"} {"question" "e-21746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of Earth and $m_2$ be the mass of the Moon. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\cdot (7.35\\times {10}^{22})}{1.98\\times {10}^{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 5.97\\cdot 7.35}{1.98}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}\\cdot {10}^{22}}{{10}^{20}} \\\\  & =(147.816…)\\times {10}^{15} \\\\  & =(14.7816…)\\times {10}^{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{14.7816…\\times {10}^{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14.7816…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{16}} \\\\  & =(3.8446…)\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between Earth and the Moon is\n$\\Big(3.84 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045949/example-21746.json" "file_sha256" "b1c0604819defc03e32f249f87a1026e617d20952e2b7635a53bef7d52925761"}] "usage" {"e-21746" [] "e-21745" [] "e-21744" []} "basis" 1687 "retractions" [[17592186216949 :question/problem "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times10^{30})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and Mars has a mass of $(6.42\\times10^{23})\\,\\textrm{kg}$.\nThe average distance between the centers of the Sun and Mars is $(2.28\\times10^{11})\\,\\textrm{m}$. According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$."] [17592186216949 :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N})$,\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg}$, and\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}$.\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of the Sun and $m_2$ be the mass of Mars. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= (6.67 \\cdot 10^{-11}) \\cdot ((1.99 \\cdot 10^{30}) \\cdot (6.42 \\cdot 10^{23}))/((2.28 \\cdot 10^{11})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (6.67 \\cdot 1.99 \\cdot 6.42)/((2.28)^{2}) \\cdot (10^{-11} \\cdot 10^{30} \\cdot 10^{23})/((10^{11})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (16.3925…) \\cdot 10^{20} \\\\\n&= (1.63925…) \\cdot 10^{21}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars is\n$\\Big(1.64 \\times 10^{21}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{N}$."] [17592186216950 :question/problem "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(3.0\\times10^3)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(3.0\\times10^{-4})\\,\\textrm{N}$. If one object has mass $(2.0\\times10^5)\\,\\textrm{kg}$, then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$."] [17592186216950 :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N})$,\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg}$, and\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}$.\n\nLet $m_2$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $m_2$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= G\\frac{m_{1}m_{2}}{r^{2}} \\\\\nFr^{2} &= Gm_{1}m_{2} \\\\\nm_{2} &= \\frac{Fr^{2}}{Gm_{1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{2} &= ((3.0 \\cdot 10^{-4}) \\cdot (3.0 \\cdot 10^{3})^{2})/((6.67 \\cdot 10^{-11}) \\cdot (2.0 \\cdot 10^{5})) \\\\\n&= (3.0 \\cdot (3.0)^{2})/(6.67 \\cdot 2.0) \\cdot (10^{-4} \\cdot (10^{3})^{2})/(10^{-11} \\cdot 10^{5}) \\\\\n&= (2.023…) \\cdot 10^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$\\Big(2.0 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{kg}$."] [17592186216951 :question/problem "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times10^{24})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and the Moon has a mass of $(7.35\\times10^{22})\\,\\textrm{kg}$. The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.98\\times10^{20})\\,\\textrm{N}$. According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of Earth and the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$."] [17592186216951 :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N})$,\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg}$, and\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}$.\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= G\\frac{m_{1}m_{2}}{r^{2}} \\\\\nr^{2} &= G\\frac{m_{1}m_{2}}{F} \\\\\nr &= \\sqrt{G\\frac{m_{1}m_{2}}{F}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of Earth and $m_2$ be the mass of the Moon. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= (6.67 \\cdot 10^{-11}) \\cdot ((5.97 \\cdot 10^{24}) \\cdot (7.35 \\cdot 10^{22}))/(1.98 \\cdot 10^{20}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6.67 \\cdot 5.97 \\cdot 7.35}{1.98} \\cdot \\frac{10^{-11} \\cdot 10^{24} \\cdot 10^{22}}{10^{20}} \\\\\n&= (147.816…) \\cdot 10^{15} \\\\\n&= (14.7816…) \\cdot 10^{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\sqrt{14.7816… \\cdot 10^{16}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{14.7816…} \\cdot \\sqrt{10^{16}} \\\\\n&= (3.8446…) \\cdot 10^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between Earth and the Moon is\n$\\Big(3.84 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{m}$."]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
